// The section "Errors and defer" of the English guide; guide.rk lists the sections.

import { Example } from "../../example.rk"

export comp Errors() {
    return <>
        <p>
            Ordinary errors need no exceptions: a function returns an error as its second value, and
            the caller checks it. An error is made with <code>error("...")</code>. With an error, a
            function of <code>(Person, error)</code> may return <code>null</code> in place of the
            value: until <code>err</code> is checked the value may be <code>null</code>, after the
            check it is not. Where the value may be <code>null</code> without an error too, the
            result is <code>(?Person, error)</code>. <code>error("load", err)</code> wraps an
            error, as <code>%w</code> does in Go: the message ends with that of <code>err</code>,
            and <code>cause</code> is <code>err</code> itself.
        </p>
        <Example name="errors" />
        <p>
            Exceptions from JS code are caught with <code>try</code> / <code>catch</code>.
            {" "}<code>defer</code> postpones a call until the function exits, which is handy for
            cleaning up: the call runs after a <code>return</code> and after an exception alike.
        </p>
        <Example name="defer" />
    </>
}
